 had won silver at Tokyo, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/141833"} "Liu Hao"] " accompanied by " [:a {:href "/athletes/141838"} "Zheng Pengfei"] ", but Zheng was no longer competing. Poland was world ranked #1 coming into Paris 2024, but their duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/145980"} "Wiktor Głazunow"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/145979"} "Tomasz Barniak"] " did not compete in this event. Germany had won the 2023 World Championships behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/125325"} "Peter Kretschmer"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/143396"} "Tim Hecker"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "At Paris, China moved up to the top step of the podium, " [:a {:href "/athletes/151201"} "Ji Bowen"] " now paddling alongside Liu, and they were not really challenged. Winning both their heat and semi-final easily, with a new Olympic best of 1:37.40 in their heat, they took the gold medal in the final by almost two seconds over the Italian pair of " [:a {:href "/athletes/151257"} "Gabriele Casadei"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/134599"} "Carlo Tacchini"] ", who took silver. Casadei and Tacchini were a last-minute substitute for the world ranked #2 crew of " [:a {:href "/athletes/151258"} "Nicolae Craciun"] " and Daniele Santini, who had won the 2021 World Championships. The unheralded Spanish boat, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/151224"} "Joan Moreno"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/151223"} "Diego Domínguez"] ", surprised by taking the bronze medal, as Kretschmer and Hecker finished fifth."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Bib"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "54/53"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/141833"} "Liu Hao"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/151201"} "Ji Bowen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-c